Layer 2: the value types every signature in ca is written with. Nothing here allocates or
touches the system; these types own nothing beyond what they hold inline.

| Area | Types |
|---|---|
| errors | `error`, `ok`, `failure`, `fail`, `result<T, E>` |
| absence | `optional<T>`, `nullopt`, `realized<T>`, the `uncertain` protocol |
| views | `span<T>` (memory), `borrowed_slice<T>` (sequences), `sv::` (text) |
| flags | `option_set`, `is_option`, `inverted`: compile-time option algebra |
| text | `ca::unicode`: decoding, validation, transcoding, character properties |
| I/O contracts | `io::is_writer`, `io::is_reader` and their buffered forms |

### Errors as values

ca has no exceptions. A function that can fail returns `ca::result<T>`: a T, or a `ca::error`
(an int code, 0 meaning success, plus the number of the trace that follows it). Failure is
built with `ca::failure{e}`, or `ca::fail(code)`; success is the value itself. Discarding a
result is a warning, and in ca's builds an error.

```c++
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/fail.hpp"
#include "ca/result.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

[[nodiscard]] static auto parse_digit(const char c) noexcept -> ca::result<int> {
	if (c < '0' || c > '9')
		return ca::failure{ca::errc::invalid_argument};
	return c - '0';
}

[[nodiscard]] static auto parse_pair(const ca::sv::str text) noexcept -> ca::result<int> {
	if (text.size() != 2U)
		return ca::fail(ca::errc::invalid_argument.code());
	auto tens = parse_digit(text[0]);
	if (!tens)
		return tens.propagate();
	auto units = parse_digit(text[1]);
	if (!units)
		return units.propagate();
	return *tens * 10 + *units;
}

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto good = parse_pair(ca::sv::str{"42"});
	if (!good || *good != 42)
		return 1;
	auto bad = parse_pair(ca::sv::str{"4x"});
	if (bad || bad.error() != ca::errc::invalid_argument)
		return 2;
	return parse_pair(ca::sv::str{"7"}).value_or(-1) == -1 ? 0 : 3;
}
```

`propagate()` hands the error on as a `ca::failure`, which converts to a result of any value
type with the same error type. `ca::result<void>` is success or an error.

### Checked access

`optional` and `result` derive `ca::uncertain`, which enforces one rule: test before you
read. A container starts unchecked; `bool`, `has_value()`, `ok()` or `holds()` check it, and
any change of its contents unchecks it again. In debug builds an unchecked `*r`, `->` or
`value()` fails: at compile time when the optimizer can see it (-O1 and above), otherwise by a
trap. Reading an absent value traps in every build. Release builds keep no flag, so the check
costs nothing there.

The functions that need no check are the ones that cannot read an absent value: `value_or`,
`realize_or`, `match`, comparisons, and the monadic chain.

```c++
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/optional.hpp"
#include "ca/result.hpp"

[[nodiscard]] static auto halve(const int n) noexcept -> ca::optional<int> {
	if (n % 2 != 0)
		return ca::nullopt;
	return n / 2;
}

[[nodiscard]] static auto checked_root(const int n) noexcept -> ca::result<int> {
	for (int r = 0; r * r <= n; ++r)
		if (r * r == n)
			return r;
	return ca::failure{ca::errc::out_of_range};
}

auto main(void) -> int {
	const int a = halve(10)
		.and_then([](const int v) noexcept { return halve(v); })
		.transform([](const int v) noexcept { return v + 1; })
		.value_or(0);
	if (a != 0)
		return 1;
	const int b = halve(8).and_then([](const int v) noexcept { return halve(v); }).value_or(0);
	if (b != 2)
		return 2;

	const int c = checked_root(17).match(
		[](const ca::realized<int>& v) noexcept { return *v; },
		[](const ca::error& e) noexcept { return -e.code(); });
	if (c >= 0)
		return 3;

	auto fixed = checked_root(17).except(ca::errc::out_of_range, [](ca::error) noexcept {
		return ca::result<int>{0};
	});
	return fixed && *fixed == 0 ? 0 : 4;
}
```

`match(on_value, on_other)` calls one of two functions: with the `realized` value, or with
the error (for `optional`, with nothing). `except(code, f)` recovers from one error and lets
the others through; `or_else`, `transform_error` and `or_fail` (optional to result) complete
the chain. `ca::realized<T>` is what a checked container converts to: a T that is known to
be there, which a function can take to state that its caller already checked.

`raise()` turns a failed result into a trap at once, for code where the error cannot be
handled locally: inside a context (`ca::with`, layer 4) the context fails with that error and
its trace; outside, the program stops.

`optional<T&>` holds a reference and never binds a temporary; assigning one rebinds it, as in
C++26.

### Views

Three views share one shape, a pointer and a size passed in two registers, and differ by what
they are for. APIs take views, never a pointer and a size.

- `ca::span<T>`: memory. Buffers, I/O, mappings. A `span<ca::byte>` also views char, signed
  char and char8_t memory, so text passes where bytes are expected.
- `ca::borrowed_slice<T>`: a sequence of elements, with the slice methods: `prefix`, `suffix`,
  `sub`, `find`, `find_if`, `contains`, `count`, `starts_with`, `compare`, `fill`, `reverse`.
  `dynamic_array`, `ca::slice` and `ca::array` share the same methods through it.
- `ca::sv::basic<C>` (`sv::str` for char): text, with Python's read-only str methods.

```c++
#include "ca/borrowed_slice.hpp"
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/span.hpp"

[[nodiscard]] static auto checksum(const ca::span<const ca::byte> data) noexcept -> unsigned {
	unsigned sum = 0U;
	for (ca::usz i = 0U; i < data.size(); ++i)
		sum += data[i];
	return sum;
}

auto main(void) -> int {
	int values[] = {5, 3, 8, 3, 1};
	const ca::borrowed_slice<int> all{values};
	if (all.count(3) != 2U || all.find(8).value_or(99U) != 2U)
		return 1;
	all.sub(1U, 3U).reverse();
	if (values[1] != 3 || values[2] != 8 || values[3] != 3)
		return 2;
	if (all.get(5U))
		return 3;

	char text[] = {'a', 'b', 'c'};
	return checksum(ca::span<char>{text}) == 'a' + 'b' + 'c' ? 0 : 4;
}
```

Constness belongs to the elements: a `span<int>` that is itself const still writes its ints.
Cutting functions clamp to the bounds rather than failing; `operator[]` traps out of range in
every build, and `get(i)` returns an optional for an index that may be out of range. A view
may be built from a temporary only when its elements are const or the temporary is itself a
view (`BORROWED`), so a view of a dying container does not compile. Arrays of const code units
are refused: a text literal's NUL would come along, and text goes through `ca::sv`.

### Option sets

`ca::option_set<Os...>` is a set of flags as a type: `a | b` is the union, `a & b` the
intersection, `~a` the complement and `a & ~b` the difference, all resolved at compile time.
Any type with a `domain`, a bit `VALUE` and a `SUPPORTED` flag is an option (`ca::is_option`).
The open flags of `ca::fs` (layer 3) are options, so `fs::descriptor` (layer 6) rejects an
unsupported or contradictory combination when it compiles.

```c++
#include "ca/fs.hpp"
#include "ca/option_set.hpp"
#include "ca/traits.hpp"

constexpr auto flags = ca::fs::append | ca::fs::cloexec | ca::fs::nonblock;
using without_nonblock = decltype(flags & ~ca::fs::nonblock);

static_assert(without_nonblock::HAS<ca::fs::append_t>);
static_assert(!without_nonblock::HAS<ca::fs::nonblock_t>);
static_assert(ca::is_same<decltype(ca::fs::append | ca::fs::append), ca::option_set<ca::fs::append_t>>);
```

`ca::in_place` selects in-place construction where a type has both a value constructor and
one taking constructor arguments.
